Rem ==================================================================
Rem devils-dust-sound.shoddy — the devil, heard.
Rem
Rem A West Riding mill ran on sound, and this file gives the devil its
Rem voice: a deep polyphonic drone that reads the LIVE Tune — not the
Rem goal — so everything built for the eyes happens for the ears. The
Rem 1875 inertia is audible for free: pull the lever to ELEVEN and the
Rem pitch climbs over seconds as the belt loads; hit STOP and the drone
Rem glides down and dies as the drum coasts to rest.
Rem
Rem The chord (voiced by SoundWave — the lab proved a low square is
Rem perceptually a click train, so the bass is sine and triangle, and
Rem the square is kept only where its edge earns its place):
Rem   ch 1  sub fundamental, SINE, ~44 Hz at NORMAL — true bass
Rem   ch 4  its detuned twin, SINE at x1.018, at FULL strength — equal
Rem         gains make the pair's beat cancel to nothing and swell back,
Rem         the deepest throb the physics allows, at ~0.8 Hz at NORMAL
Rem         and quickening with the machine (the lab's detuned pair,
Rem         transcribed and turned up)
Rem   ch 2  the fifth above, TRIANGLE, quieter — warmth over the sub
Rem   ch 3  the overload voice — a TRIANGLE octave while the machine is
Rem         calm (the whole bed hums), but when the live values run hot
Rem         it turns SQUARE and slides to a flat ninth: the buzz IS the
Rem         overload, arriving and leaving with the ember on the gauges
Rem   ch 5  lever gestures (short MML phrases), driven by the window
Rem   ch 6  a soft pat when wool reaches the floor
Rem
Rem The rhythm is the CHURN: the drone's gain breathes once per drum
Rem revolution, so the tempo IS the machine speed — and because it is
Rem pure gain modulation on held notes, it cannot tick. (Its ancestor,
Rem a queued sub-bass kick, read as morse code on small speakers: a
Rem 40 Hz square's fundamental is inaudible there, leaving only clicky
Rem harmonics at whatever rate the drum happened to turn.)
Rem
Rem Everything here is fire-and-forget — sound never blocks — and the
Rem mapping half (DroneHz, PartialHz, AnyHot, BedGain, RevCrossed) is
Rem PURE, tested headless in test.shoddy. Only MillVoice and its
Rem helpers touch the buzzer, and they are no-ops on a silent machine.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "devils-dust-core.shoddy"
Include "buzzer.shoddy"
Include "math.shoddy"

Rem ---- the pure mapping ----------------------------------------------

Rem The drone fundamental from the live knobs: 44 Hz at the NORMAL drum
Rem speed on a deliberately FLAT power curve — about 30 Hz at SLOW
Rem (audible, above the gate), ~65 at MAX, ~70 at eleven. A machine's
Rem hum rises with load; it does not leap octaves. (An earlier steeper
Rem curve both silenced SLOW below the gate and doubled the pitch at
Rem MAX.) Falls toward nothing as the drum winds down.
Def DroneHz(kb As Knobs) As Number
    44 * (DrumStep(kb) / 0.264) ^ 0.35

Rem Below this the mill is considered silent — the drone gates off part
Rem way down a STOP coast, which is the dying fall.
Def DroneGate() As Number
    20

Rem The overload voice's ratio: a clean octave, or a flat ninth (an
Rem octave and a semitone) when the machine runs past its rails.
Def PartialHz(f As Number, hot As Boolean) As Number
    Ifte(hot, f * 2.1189, f * 2)

Rem Is any gauge past its rail? Eleven's signature.
Def AnyHot(kb As Knobs) As Boolean
    Any(Range(1, 21), Fn(i) => KnobHot(kb, i))

Rem The drone bed's loudness follows the drum: strong at NORMAL, a
Rem little fuller as the machine loads — the slope is gentle because
Rem the pitch already tells the speed story. Sine and triangle carry
Rem far less energy than the squares this scale was first tuned for —
Rem these gains look loud on paper and are not.
Def BedGain(kb As Knobs) As Number
    Clamp(0.6 + 0.25 * DrumStep(kb) / 0.264, 0, 0.95)

Rem The churn: a whisper of breath at the drum's rate, between 0.84
Rem and 1 of full bed gain. It stays a whisper on purpose — its rate is
Rem locked to drum revolutions, and at MAX that is nearly 4 a second: a
Rem deep churn there reads as harsh tremolo, not breathing. The real
Rem throb is the detuned pair's beat, which is amplitude-deep and
Rem speed-scaled by physics rather than by modulation.
Def Churn(turn As Number) As Number
    0.92 + 0.08 * Sin(turn)

Rem ---- the effectful half --------------------------------------------

Rem The orchestration, set once at startup (SoundWave is sticky like
Rem SoundGain): sine sub, triangle fifth, triangle overload voice —
Rem VoiceBed turns it square while the machine runs hot — triangle
Rem lever gestures, sine floor pats.
Def VoiceWaves()
    SoundWave(1, WaveSine())
    SoundWave(2, WaveTriangle())
    SoundWave(3, WaveTriangle())
    SoundWave(4, WaveSine())
    SoundWave(5, WaveTriangle())
    SoundWave(6, WaveSine())

Rem Kill the drone chord. Idempotent: lastHz 0 means nothing was held.
Def BedOff(lastHz As Number) As Number
    If lastHz > 0 Then
        NoteOff(1)
        NoteOff(2)
        NoteOff(3)
        NoteOff(4)
        0
    Else
        0

Rem Full silence: chord off and every effect channel stopped. Used by
Rem the mute key and at quit.
Def AllQuiet(lastHz As Number) As Number
    SoundStop(5)
    SoundStop(6)
    BedOff(lastHz)

Rem Retune the drone chord. Gains follow every frame — cheap, and this
Rem is where the churn lives: the fundamental and fifth breathe with
Rem the drum angle while the overload partial holds steady above them
Rem (contrast makes the sour note carry). Pitches resend only on retrig
Rem (the fundamental moved) or rehot (the chord soured or resolved) —
Rem NoteOn re-pitches a held note.
Def VoiceBed(kb As Knobs, f As Number, retrig As Boolean, rehot As Boolean, turn As Number)
    SoundGain(1, BedGain(kb) * Churn(turn))
    SoundGain(4, BedGain(kb) * Churn(turn))
    SoundGain(2, BedGain(kb) * 0.5 * Churn(turn))
    SoundGain(3, BedGain(kb) * Ifte(AnyHot(kb), 0.6, 0.28))
    SoundWave(3, Ifte(AnyHot(kb), WaveSquare(), WaveTriangle()))
    If retrig Then
        NoteOn(1, f)
        NoteOn(4, f * 1.018)
        NoteOn(2, f * 1.5)
    If retrig Or rehot Then
        NoteOn(3, PartialHz(f, AnyHot(kb)))

Rem A soft distant pat when wool reaches the floor, on its own quiet
Rem channel, pitched where small speakers can actually voice it.
Rem Throws off the rim make no sound at all — the churn carries the
Rem machine's rhythm — and nothing here may use the anonymous pool:
Rem Sound() plays at a FIXED effect volume with no gain control, which
Rem turned every throw into a loud stream of morse code.
Def LandPat(before As Dust, after As Dust)
    If Landed(TakeCensus(Fibers(after))) > Landed(TakeCensus(Fibers(before))) Then
        SoundGain(6, 0.1)
        SoundQueue(6, 85, 100)

Rem One frame of the mill's voice: pats always, the churning drone
Rem chord when the drum turns fast enough to hum. Returns the drone Hz
Rem actually sounding (0 = gated off) so the caller can rate-limit the
Rem next retune.
Def MillVoice(before As Dust, after As Dust, lastHz As Number) As Number
    Let kb = Tune(after)
    LandPat(before, after)
    Let f = DroneHz(kb)
    If f < DroneGate() Then
        BedOff(lastHz)
    Else
        Let f2 = Ifte(lastHz > 0 And Abs(f - lastHz) < lastHz * 0.03, lastHz, f)
        VoiceBed(kb, f2, f2 <> lastHz, AnyHot(kb) <> AnyHot(Tune(before)), Whirl(after))
        f2
